 ## Overview
-Regularization and Loss Functions define the mathematical objectives used to constrain the optimization of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) beyond standard photometric reconstruction. They solve the problem of geometric ambiguity, overfitting to incorrect surfaces, and artifacts such as "floaters" or color over-saturation, which are particularly prevalent in sparse-view settings or texture-less regions where photometric loss alone is insufficient [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. This family sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *objective* terms added to the total loss rather than the *sampling* strategy, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by providing the specific gradient signals that drive parameter updates.
+Regularization and Loss Functions define the objective landscape for optimizing Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), extending the standard photometric reconstruction loss to address geometric ambiguity, overfitting, and semantic inconsistency, particularly in sparse-view or ill-posed scenarios [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. Unlike standard photometric optimization which minimizes pixel-wise color differences, this family incorporates geometric priors, structural similarity, semantic embeddings, and generative diffusion models to constrain the density and color fields [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *what* to optimize (the loss terms) rather than the *how* of sampling, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by providing the mathematical definitions of the error metrics rather than hyperparameter schedules.
 
